WEBVTT

00:00.384 --> 00:02.050
In the last video,
we learned that if we

00:02.050 --> 00:05.035
have two different
triangles and if all

00:05.035 --> 00:08.060
of the corresponding
sides of the two triangles

00:08.060 --> 00:11.770
have the same length,
then by side-side-side,

00:11.770 --> 00:14.640
we know that the two
triangles are congruent.

00:14.640 --> 00:16.580
And I also touched a
little bit on the idea

00:16.580 --> 00:18.030
of an axiom or postulate.

00:18.030 --> 00:19.230
But I want to be clear.

00:19.230 --> 00:21.070
Sometimes you will
hear this referred

00:21.070 --> 00:25.880
to as a side-side-side
theorem, and sometimes you'll

00:25.880 --> 00:30.900
hear it as a side-side-side
postulate or axiom.

00:30.900 --> 00:33.090
And I think it's
worth differentiating

00:33.090 --> 00:34.700
what these mean.

00:34.700 --> 00:36.200
A postulate or an
axiom is something

00:36.200 --> 00:38.430
that you just assume, you
assume from the get-go,

00:38.430 --> 00:42.480
while a theorem is something
you prove using more basic

00:42.480 --> 00:46.050
or using some
postulates or axioms.

00:46.050 --> 00:50.660
Really in all of mathematics,
you make some core assumptions.

00:50.660 --> 00:54.010
You call these the
axioms or the postulates.

00:59.480 --> 01:02.900
And then using those, you
try to prove theorems.

01:02.900 --> 01:07.030
So maybe using that one, I can
prove some theorem over here.

01:07.030 --> 01:09.610
And maybe using that
theorem, and then this axiom,

01:09.610 --> 01:11.880
I can prove another
theorem over here.

01:11.880 --> 01:13.900
And then using both
of those theorems,

01:13.900 --> 01:15.640
I can prove another
theorem over here.

01:15.640 --> 01:16.806
I think you get the picture.

01:16.806 --> 01:19.470
This axiom might lead
us to this theorem.

01:19.470 --> 01:23.290
These two might lead us to
this theorem right over here.

01:23.290 --> 01:26.110
And we essentially try
to build our knowledge

01:26.110 --> 01:30.110
or we build a mathematics
around these core assumptions.

01:30.110 --> 01:33.270
In an introductory
geometry class,

01:33.270 --> 01:38.967
we don't rigorously prove
the side-side-side theorem.

01:38.967 --> 01:40.800
And that's why in a lot
of geometry classes,

01:40.800 --> 01:42.520
you kind of just
take it as a given,

01:42.520 --> 01:44.136
as a postulate or an axiom.

01:44.136 --> 01:45.760
And the whole reason
why I'm doing this

01:45.760 --> 01:48.134
is one, just so you know the
difference between the words

01:48.134 --> 01:50.490
theorem and postulate or axiom.

01:50.490 --> 01:52.209
And also so that you
don't get confused.

01:52.209 --> 01:54.000
It is just a given,
but in a lot of books--

01:54.000 --> 01:55.416
and I've looked
at several books--

01:55.416 --> 01:57.629
they do refer to it as the
side-side-side theorem,

01:57.629 --> 01:59.420
even though they never
prove it rigorously.

01:59.420 --> 02:00.600
They do just assume it.

02:00.600 --> 02:03.690
So it really is more of
a postulate or an axiom.

02:03.690 --> 02:05.494
Now with that out
of the way, we're

02:05.494 --> 02:06.910
just going to
assume going forward

02:06.910 --> 02:08.410
that we just know
that this is true.

02:08.410 --> 02:09.820
We're going to
take it as a given.

02:09.820 --> 02:11.403
I want to show you
that we can already

02:11.403 --> 02:13.507
do something pretty
useful with it.

02:13.507 --> 02:14.965
So let's say that
we have a circle.

02:17.530 --> 02:20.710
And there's many useful things
that we can already do with it.

02:20.710 --> 02:24.720
And this circle has a
center, right here at A.

02:24.720 --> 02:28.870
And let's say that we have
a chord in the circle that

02:28.870 --> 02:30.440
is not a diameter.

02:30.440 --> 02:32.780
So let me draw a chord here.

02:32.780 --> 02:35.150
So let me draw a
chord in this circle.

02:35.150 --> 02:38.350
So it's kind of a
segment of a secant line.

02:38.350 --> 02:42.690
And let's say that
I have a line that

02:42.690 --> 02:47.473
bisects this chord
from the center.

02:47.473 --> 02:49.223
And I guess I call it
a radius because I'm

02:49.223 --> 02:53.600
going to go from the center
to the edge of the circle

02:53.600 --> 02:54.570
right over there.

02:54.570 --> 02:57.830
So I'm going to the center
to the circle itself.

02:57.830 --> 02:59.760
And when I say bisects
it-- so these are all--

02:59.760 --> 03:01.870
I'm just setting up
the problem right now.

03:01.870 --> 03:03.490
When I say bisecting
it, it means

03:03.490 --> 03:05.830
it splits that line
segment in half.

03:05.830 --> 03:08.560
So what it tells is, is that
the length of this segment

03:08.560 --> 03:11.400
right over here is going to
be equivalent to the length

03:11.400 --> 03:15.170
of this segment
right over there.

03:15.170 --> 03:15.795
I've set it up.

03:15.795 --> 03:16.910
I have a circle.

03:16.910 --> 03:20.380
This radius bisects this
chord right over here.

03:20.380 --> 03:27.720
And the goal here
is to prove that it

03:27.720 --> 03:29.909
bisects this chord
at a right angle.

03:29.909 --> 03:32.200
Or another way to say it--
let me add some points here.

03:32.200 --> 03:35.890
Let's call this B.
Let's call this C,

03:35.890 --> 03:38.940
And let's call this
D. I want to prove

03:38.940 --> 03:43.290
that segment AB
is perpendicular.

03:43.290 --> 03:45.030
It intersects it
at a right angle.

03:45.030 --> 03:48.170
It is perpendicular
to segment CD.

03:50.682 --> 03:52.140
And as you could
imagine, I'm going

03:52.140 --> 03:55.790
to prove it pretty much using
the side-side-side whatever you

03:55.790 --> 03:59.220
want to call it, side-side-side
theorem, postulate, or axiom.

03:59.220 --> 04:00.001
So let's do it.

04:00.001 --> 04:01.250
Let's think about it this way.

04:01.250 --> 04:02.610
So you can imagine if
I'm going to use this,

04:02.610 --> 04:03.859
I need to have some triangles.

04:03.859 --> 04:05.630
There's no triangles
here right now.

04:05.630 --> 04:08.130
But I can construct triangles,
and I can construct triangles

04:08.130 --> 04:09.640
based on things I know.

04:09.640 --> 04:14.105
For example, I can construct--
this has some radius.

04:16.740 --> 04:18.074
That's a radius right over here.

04:18.074 --> 04:20.739
The length of that is just going
to be the radius of the circle.

04:20.739 --> 04:22.390
But I can also do
it right over here.

04:22.390 --> 04:26.520
The length of AC is also going
to be the radius of the circle.

04:26.520 --> 04:29.450
So we know that these two lines
have the same length, which

04:29.450 --> 04:32.070
is the radius of the circle.

04:32.070 --> 04:34.500
Or we could say that
AD is congruent to AC,

04:34.500 --> 04:36.990
or they have the
exact same lengths.

04:36.990 --> 04:40.380
We know from the
set-up in the problem

04:40.380 --> 04:43.310
that this segment
is equal in length

04:43.310 --> 04:45.310
to this segment over here.

04:45.310 --> 04:47.500
Let me add a point here
so I can refer to it.

04:47.500 --> 04:49.310
So if I call that
point E, we know

04:49.310 --> 04:54.210
from the set-up in the problem,
that CE is congruent to ED,

04:54.210 --> 04:55.620
or they have the same lengths.

04:55.620 --> 04:58.260
CE has the same length as ED.

04:58.260 --> 05:01.700
And we also know that both of
these triangles, the one here

05:01.700 --> 05:06.900
on the left and the one here
on the right, they both share

05:06.900 --> 05:08.410
the side EA.

05:08.410 --> 05:11.540
So EA is clearly equal to EA.

05:11.540 --> 05:13.990
So this is clearly
equal to itself.

05:13.990 --> 05:14.790
It's the same side.

05:14.790 --> 05:16.820
The same side is being
used for both triangles.

05:16.820 --> 05:19.240
The triangles are
adjacent to each other.

05:19.240 --> 05:23.250
And so we see a situation where
we have two different triangles

05:23.250 --> 05:26.220
that have corresponding
sides being equal.

05:26.220 --> 05:29.230
This side is equivalent to
this side right over here.

05:29.230 --> 05:33.760
This side is equal in length
to that side over there.

05:33.760 --> 05:37.364
And then, obviously, AE
is equivalent to itself.

05:37.364 --> 05:38.530
It's a side on both of them.

05:38.530 --> 05:41.820
It's the corresponding side
on both of these triangles.

05:41.820 --> 06:05.440
And so by side-side-side, AEC.

06:05.440 --> 06:07.140
Let me write it over here.

06:07.140 --> 06:17.304
By side-side-side, we
know that triangle AEC

06:17.304 --> 06:19.940
is congruent to triangle AED.

06:24.080 --> 06:25.500
But how does that help us?

06:25.500 --> 06:27.490
How does that help
us knowing that we

06:27.490 --> 06:29.110
used our little theorem?

06:29.110 --> 06:31.860
But how does that
actually help us here?

06:31.860 --> 06:34.430
Well, what's cool is once we
know that two triangles are

06:34.430 --> 06:41.050
congruent-- so because they
are congruent, from that,

06:41.050 --> 06:43.470
we can deduce that all
the angles are the same.

06:43.470 --> 06:45.950
And in particular,
we can deduce that

06:45.950 --> 06:53.270
this angle right over here,
that the measure of angle CEA

06:53.270 --> 06:55.806
is equivalent to the
measure of angle DEA.

07:00.860 --> 07:03.900
And the reason why that's
useful is that we also

07:03.900 --> 07:05.680
see, just by looking
at this, that they're

07:05.680 --> 07:06.905
supplementary to each other.

07:06.905 --> 07:07.920
They're adjacent angles.

07:07.920 --> 07:10.830
Their outer sides
form a straight angle.

07:10.830 --> 07:16.830
So CEA is supplementary
and equivalent to DEA.

07:16.830 --> 07:18.790
So, they're are
also supplementary.

07:18.790 --> 07:24.480
So we also have the
measure of angle CEA

07:24.480 --> 07:30.030
plus the measure of angle
DEA is equal to 180 degrees.

07:30.030 --> 07:31.730
But they're equivalent
to each other.

07:31.730 --> 07:35.090
So I could replace the measure
of DEA with the measure of CEA.

07:37.690 --> 07:42.830
Or I could rewrite this as 2
times the measure of angle CEA

07:42.830 --> 07:45.110
is equal to 180 degrees.

07:45.110 --> 07:47.130
Or I could divide
both sides by 2.

07:47.130 --> 07:49.870
And I say the
measure of angle CEA

07:49.870 --> 07:52.180
is equal to 90
degrees, which is going

07:52.180 --> 07:54.710
to be the same as the
measure of angle DEA

07:54.710 --> 07:56.820
because they're equivalent.

07:56.820 --> 07:59.495
So we know that this angle
right over here is 90 degrees.

07:59.495 --> 08:01.600
So I can do it with
that little box.

08:01.600 --> 08:04.540
And this angle right
over here is 90 degrees.

08:04.540 --> 08:10.290
And because AB intersects
where it intersects CD,

08:10.290 --> 08:12.290
we have a 90 degree
angle here and there.

08:12.290 --> 08:14.900
And we could also prove
this over there as well.

08:14.900 --> 08:19.090
They are perpendicular
to each other.
